<CapaProblem id="uk_mc_date_place" title="Magna Carta (1215)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>When and where did King John seal Magna Carta?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>15 June 1215, at Runnymede (between Windsor and Staines)</Key>
      <Distractor>12 June 1215, at Westminster</Distractor>
      <Distractor>15 June 1215, at the Tower of London</Distractor>
      <Distractor>15 June 1216, at Runnymede</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>King John set his seal to the draft charter on 15 June 1215 in the meadow called Runnymede, between Windsor and Staines, beside the Thames.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The charter itself is dated 'the fifteenth day of June in the seventeenth year of our reign'. Talks began about 10 June; 15 June is the sealing date.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/magna-carta-1214-1225/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_mc_why_1215" title="Magna Carta (1215)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Why was Magna Carta forced on King John, and who led the rebel barons?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Over John's wish to change the religion of England; Simon de Montfort led them</Distractor>
      <Key>Barons revolted over John's heavy financial demands and the failed 1214 French campaign; Robert fitz Walter led them</Key>
      <Distractor>Over a lost war with Scotland; William Marshal led them</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Magna Carta followed a baronial revolt after King John's failed French campaign of 1214 and his heavy financial demands; the rebel barons renounced their allegiance on 5 May 1215 and named Robert fitz Walter (Fitzwalter) as their leader.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Wikipedia also credits Archbishop Stephen Langton with first drafting the charter.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/magna-carta-1214-1225/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_mc_25_barons" title="Magna Carta (1215)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>How many barons were to enforce the 1215 charter, and what could they do if the king broke it?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>12 barons; they could depose him</Distractor>
      <Distractor>63 barons; they could exile him</Distractor>
      <Key>25 barons; after 40 days of non-compliance they could seize his castles and lands</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The 1215 charter created a council of 25 barons to monitor the king's compliance; if he failed to comply within 40 days they could seize his castles and lands.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>National Archives page confirms 'the 25 barons'; the 40-day clause is from Wikipedia only.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magna_Carta (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_mc_clause_39_40" title="Magna Carta (1215)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Which clause of Magna Carta promises 'We will not sell, or deny, or delay right or justice to anyone'?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>Clause 40 (clause 39 is the lawful-judgment-of-peers clause)</Key>
      <Distractor>Clause 63</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Clause 1</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Clause 39 of 1215 says no free man is to be arrested, imprisoned, disseised, outlawed or exiled, nor proceeded against, except by the lawful judgment of his peers or by the law of the land. Clause 40 says: 'We will not sell, or deny, or delay right or justice to anyone.'</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Translation from the Magna Carta Project (UEA). The clause numbers are not medieval: the 63-clause division was introduced by William Blackstone in 1759 (search-result claim, not opened at source).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://magnacarta.cmp.uea.ac.uk/read/magna_carta_1215/Clause_40 (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_mc_reissues" title="Magna Carta (1215)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Who annulled Magna Carta in 1215, and in which year did Edward I confirm it as statute law?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Archbishop Langton; 1225</Distractor>
      <Key>Pope Innocent III annulled it; Edward I confirmed it in 1297</Key>
      <Distractor>Pope Clement VII; 1327</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Pope Innocent III declared the charter null and void in August 1215. It was reissued in 1216 and 1217 (under the regency for the young Henry III) and in the definitive version of 11 February 1225; Edward I confirmed it as statute law in 1297.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Reissue dates per National Archives: 12 Nov 1216, 6 Nov 1217, 11 Feb 1225. 1297 per Wikipedia (Magna Carta).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/magna-carta-1214-1225/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_mc_what_changed" title="Magna Carta (1215)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What did Magna Carta do to the king's power?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>It abolished the monarchy</Distractor>
      <Distractor>It created the House of Commons</Distractor>
      <Key>It bound the king to the law: limits on feudal payments, protection of the Church, and no punishment of free men except by lawful judgment</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Magna Carta protected the freedom of the Church, capped the relief (inheritance payment) of an earl's barony at 100 pounds and a knight's fee at 100 shillings, and framed royal power within established rules rather than the king's personal will. Three or four 1215 clauses (including 39 and 40) survived in English law.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Sources conflict on how many 1215 clauses survive: 'three' (1, 39, 40 in the 1297 version per Wikipedia) versus 'four' (1, 13, 39, 40). Do not examine the exact count.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: http://www.nationalarchives.gov.uk/education/teaching-resources/magna-carta-1214-1225/ (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_mc_surviving_copies" title="Magna Carta (1215)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>How many copies of the 1215 Magna Carta survive, and where?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>Four: two in the British Library, one at Lincoln Castle, one at Salisbury Cathedral</Key>
      <Distractor>Two: Westminster Abbey and the Tower</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Seven, one per baron</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Four exemplifications of the 1215 charter survive: two at the British Library, one at Lincoln Castle and one at Salisbury Cathedral. All four were displayed together at the British Library in 2015 for the 800th anniversary.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>National Archives page confirms the Salisbury original is not held by the Archives. Counts and locations cross-checked in several secondary sources; the British Library page was not reachable.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magna_Carta (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_supremacy_what" title="Act of Supremacy (1534)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What title did the 1534 Act of Supremacy give Henry VIII?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Defender of the Faith</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Supreme Governor of the Church of England</Distractor>
      <Key>Supreme Head of the Church of England</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Act of Supremacy 1534 (26 Hen. 8 c. 1) declared Henry VIII 'the only supreme head on Earth of the Church of England', asserting its independence from the Pope.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>'Supreme Governor' is the Elizabethan title (1559) and a common distractor. 'Defender of the Faith' was a papal title granted to Henry in 1521 (background knowledge).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_Supremacy_1534 (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_supremacy_date" title="Act of Supremacy (1534)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>In which year was the (first) Act of Supremacy passed?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>1533</Distractor>
      <Distractor>1559</Distractor>
      <Distractor>1536</Distractor>
      <Key>1534 (November session, royal assent 18 December 1534)</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Act was passed by the Parliament session that opened 3 November 1534 and received royal assent on 18 December 1534, according to Wikipedia's infobox.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Exam will test only the year. The 3 November / 18 December detail rests on Wikipedia alone; Britannica and parliament.uk were not reachable. Some sources give 'November 1534' for passing.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_Supremacy_1534 (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_supremacy_why" title="Act of Supremacy (1534)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Why did Henry VIII break with Rome?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Pope had excommunicated him over a war with France</Distractor>
      <Key>The Pope would not annul his marriage to Catherine of Aragon, so Henry made himself head of the Church of England</Key>
      <Distractor>He had converted to Lutheranism</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Pope Clement VII refused to annul Henry VIII's marriage to Catherine of Aragon, arguing the marriage was indissoluble and that an earlier dispensation could not simply be set aside. The Act of Supremacy made the king, not the Pope, head of the English Church, which let Henry settle his 'Great Matter' at home.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_Supremacy_1534 (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_supremacy_treason" title="Act of Supremacy (1534)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What happened to people, such as Thomas More, who refused to accept the king's supremacy?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>It was treason; More was beheaded in 1535</Key>
      <Distractor>They were excommunicated by the Pope only</Distractor>
      <Distractor>They were fined and exiled</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Treasons Act 1534 (26 Hen. 8 c. 13) made it treason to deny the king his 'dignity, title or name', so refusing the Supremacy became a capital crime. Thomas More, who refused the oath, was convicted of treason and beheaded (6 July 1535).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Wikipedia page fetched confirms the Treasons Act and More's beheading but gave no date; 6 July 1535 is background knowledge, to be checked against the Tower or Britannica.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_Supremacy_1534 (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_supremacy_repeal_eliz" title="Act of Supremacy (1534)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>How did Elizabeth I's title in the Church differ from her father's?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>She was Defender of the Faith only</Distractor>
      <Key>She was Supreme Governor, not Supreme Head, of the Church of England</Key>
      <Distractor>She was Head of the Church, same as Henry</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Mary I repealed the Act (See of Rome Act, 12 November 1554). Elizabeth I's Act of Supremacy of 1558/1559 (1 Eliz. 1 c. 1) made her 'Supreme Governor', not Supreme Head, of the Church of England, and introduced an Oath of Supremacy.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Elizabethan Act is officially dated 1558 (Old Style) though passed in April 1559; Wikipedia gives royal assent 8 May 1559. Repeal date given as 12 November 1554 on the page.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Act_of_Supremacy_1534 (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_gfl_dates_start" title="Great Fire of London (1666)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>When did the Great Fire of London burn, and where did it start?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>12-15 September 1666; Fetter Lane</Distractor>
      <Distractor>2-5 September 1665; a tavern in Pie Corner</Distractor>
      <Key>2-5 September 1666; Thomas Farriner's bakery in Pudding Lane</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Great Fire ran from Sunday 2 to Wednesday 5 September 1666. It began shortly after midnight in the bakery of Thomas Farriner in Pudding Lane.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Julian calendar. Monument site (themonument.org.uk/history) confirms Farriner's bakery, Pudding Lane.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_London (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_gfl_bloodworth" title="Great Fire of London (1666)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Who was Lord Mayor of London during the Great Fire, and why was he criticised?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Christopher Wren; he blew up the wrong street</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Samuel Pepys; he refused to open the City gates</Distractor>
      <Key>Thomas Bloodworth; he hesitated to pull down houses as firebreaks and dismissed the fire</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Lord Mayor, Thomas Bloodworth, was criticised for delaying demolitions of houses to make firebreaks. When pressed he reportedly said 'A woman might piss it out' and left.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The quote is reported by Pepys and later writers; wording varies. Do not examine the quote.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_London (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_gfl_damage" title="Great Fire of London (1666)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Roughly how many houses and parish churches did the Great Fire destroy?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>About 1,300 houses and 8 churches</Distractor>
      <Distractor>About 130,000 houses and 87 churches</Distractor>
      <Key>About 13,200 houses and 87 parish churches (plus St Paul's)</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>About 13,200-13,500 houses, 87 parish churches (some sources say 86), Old St Paul's Cathedral, the Royal Exchange and 44 Livery Company halls were destroyed. Wikipedia reckons about 15 per cent of the City's housing.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Counts vary: 13,200 houses is the traditional figure; churches 86 or 87. Accept 13,200 and 87 (or about 13,000 and about 87).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_London (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_gfl_ending" title="Great Fire of London (1666)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What finally stopped the Great Fire?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Thames was pumped over the City by the Navy</Distractor>
      <Distractor>A heavy rainstorm</Distractor>
      <Key>The wind dropped and firebreaks were made by demolition and gunpowder blasts</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The wind dropped on Tuesday evening, and demolition and gunpowder explosions by the Tower garrison created firebreaks that stopped the fire on Wednesday. The Golden Boy of Pye Corner in Smithfield marks where the fire is said to have ended.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Pye Corner marker tradition: the boy was once read as blaming gluttony, since the Fire was seen by some as a punishment.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_London (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_gfl_pepys" title="Great Fire of London (1666)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Which diarist described the Fire as 'the saddest sight of desolation that I ever saw'?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Christopher Wren</Distractor>
      <Distractor>John Evelyn</Distractor>
      <Key>Samuel Pepys</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Samuel Pepys watched from Barking Church tower and described it as 'the saddest sight of desolation that I ever saw'. John Evelyn also kept a record. Both diaries are the main eyewitness sources.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Pepys's diary entry for 5 September 1666; the Wikipedia article attributes the viewing to Barking Church. Pepys is known for burying his parmesan cheese and wine in a pit; background knowledge.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_London (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_gfl_blame_hubert" title="Great Fire of London (1666)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Who was hanged for starting the Great Fire, and was he guilty?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Thomas Farriner, the baker; yes</Distractor>
      <Key>Robert Hubert, a French watchmaker; almost certainly not guilty</Key>
      <Distractor>Guy Fawkes; yes</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Londoners blamed foreigners, especially the French, Dutch and Catholics, in the middle of the Second Anglo-Dutch War. Robert Hubert, a French watchmaker, falsely confessed and was hanged on 27 October 1666; later evidence showed he had been at sea and arrived two days after the fire began.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Round-two facts review: British Library gives Tyburn, 27 October 1666. The Wikipedia Great Fire article gives 29 October; use the British Library date. Robert Hubert article and other sources agree with 27 October.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.bl.uk/stories/blogs/posts/robert-hubert-and-the-great-fire-of-london (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_gfl_rebuilding" title="Great Fire of London (1666)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What materials did the rebuilt City use instead of timber, and who designed St Paul's after the Fire?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Iron and glass; Inigo Jones</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Timber again; Nicholas Hawksmoor</Distractor>
      <Key>Brick and stone; Christopher Wren designed the new St Paul's</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Radical rebuilding plans by Wren and Evelyn were not carried out; the old street plan was largely recreated, with brick and stone replacing timber. The Rebuilding of London Act 1666 and a Fire Court (1667-76) settled disputes. The Monument was built 1671-77, and its inscription blaming Catholics was removed in 1830.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Wren as designer of the new St Paul's is background knowledge not stated in the fetched excerpt. Wikipedia lists the Fire of London Disputes Act 1666 and the Rebuilding of London Acts 1666 and 1670; it does not mention a 1667 Act.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Fire_of_London (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_suez_trigger" title="Suez Crisis (1956)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What did Nasser do on 26 July 1956 that started the Suez Crisis?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>He expelled the British ambassador</Distractor>
      <Key>He nationalised the Suez Canal Company</Key>
      <Distractor>He closed the Straits of Tiran</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>On 26 July 1956 Egyptian President Gamal Abdel Nasser nationalised the Suez Canal Company, shortly after the US withdrew funding for the Aswan Dam.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The Canal opened in 1869; Britain bought a 44 per cent stake in 1875 and occupied Egypt in 1882. The 1954 Anglo-Egyptian agreement set the phased withdrawal of British troops.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Crisis (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_suez_sevres" title="Suez Crisis (1956)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What was the secret Protocol of Sevres?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>A treaty with Egypt on shared canal control</Distractor>
      <Distractor>A secret US-UK arms deal</Distractor>
      <Key>A secret Britain-France-Israel plan: Israel attacks Egypt, then Britain and France intervene under cover of an ultimatum</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Protocol of Sevres (22-24 October 1956) was a secret plan between Britain, France and Israel: Israel would attack Egypt, then Britain and France would issue an ultimatum and intervene as 'peacekeepers' to protect the Canal.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>'Collusion' is the historians' term; the British government denied it in Parliament.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Crisis (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_suez_timeline" title="Suez Crisis (1956)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Which dates marked the Israeli invasion and the British-French ceasefire?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>Israel invaded 29 October 1956; the ceasefire was 6 November 1956</Key>
      <Distractor>26 July; 6 December</Distractor>
      <Distractor>5 November; 6 November</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Israel invaded Sinai on 29 October 1956 (Operation Kadesh). Britain and France issued their ultimatum on 30 October and began bombing on 31 October; Anglo-French paratroopers landed at Port Said on 5 November and Royal Marine commandos on 6 November. Eden announced a ceasefire on 6 November.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Crisis (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_suez_pressure" title="Suez Crisis (1956)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What forced Britain to stop the Suez operation?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Egyptian military victory</Distractor>
      <Key>American financial and diplomatic pressure (a run on sterling), plus UN and Soviet pressure</Key>
      <Distractor>A vote of the House of Lords</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The US (Eisenhower) refused to support the invasion and prepared to sell its holdings of sterling bonds, a run on the pound that Chancellor Harold Macmillan warned Eden Britain's reserves could not withstand. The Soviet Union also threatened. The UN established its first Emergency Force (UNEF); Canadian Lester Pearson won the Nobel Peace Prize for the diplomacy.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The Hungarian Revolution (October-November 1956) occurred at the same time.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Crisis (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_suez_eden" title="Suez Crisis (1956)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Which Prime Minister fell because of Suez, and who followed him?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Clement Attlee; Harold Wilson</Distractor>
      <Key>Anthony Eden (resigned 9 January 1957); Harold Macmillan succeeded him</Key>
      <Distractor>Winston Churchill; Anthony Eden</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Prime Minister Anthony Eden resigned on 9 January 1957 and was succeeded by Harold Macmillan. Eden was accused of misleading Parliament.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Ill health is often cited as Eden's reason; the Wikipedia page cites misleading Parliament. Macmillan's appointment (10 January) is background knowledge.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Crisis (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_suez_casualties_significance" title="Suez Crisis (1956)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What did Suez reveal about Britain's place in the world?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>Britain could no longer act independently of the United States; the end of its imperial great-power role</Key>
      <Distractor>Britain had retained full control over Egypt</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Commonwealth had broken up</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Casualties: Britain 22 killed and 96 wounded, France 10 killed, Israel 172 killed, Egyptian military deaths about 1,650-3,000. The Canal was blocked and reopened by April 1957. The crisis showed Britain and France could not act without US consent and is read as the end of Britain's role as a first-rank imperial power.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Wikipedia gives 'canal closed from October 1956 to March 1957' in one place and reopened to shipping by 24 April 1957 in another. Treat 'spring 1957'.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suez_Crisis (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_falk_invasion" title="Falklands War (1982)" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>When did Argentina invade the Falklands?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>19 March 1982</Distractor>
      <Distractor>2 May 1982</Distractor>
      <Distractor>14 June 1982</Distractor>
      <Key>2 April 1982</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Argentine forces invaded the Falkland Islands on 2 April 1982 and South Georgia the next day. Neither state formally declared war. The British government sent a naval task force on 5 April.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The cabinet decision to send the Task Force was taken on 2 April (Thatcher Foundation catalogue of National Archives release, not opened). Operation Corporate was the British name.</Markdown>
