<CapaProblem id="uk_tower_keys" title="The Tower of London" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What does the Chief Warder answer when the sentry asks 'Whose keys?'</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>'King Charles's keys' (the reigning monarch's name)</Key>
      <Distractor>'The Crown Jewels' keys'</Distractor>
      <Distractor>'The Constable's keys'</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Ceremony of the Keys locks up the Tower every night. The Chief Yeoman Warder, carrying a candle lantern, leaves the Byward Tower at 9.52 pm with an escort; at the Bloody Tower archway the sentry challenges 'Who comes there?' and the Warder answers 'The keys', 'Whose keys?', 'King Charles's keys'. On the 'Amen' the clock strikes ten and the Last Post is sounded.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Hrp page said to confirm the ceremony takes place 'each and every night, without fail, for at least 700 years' (search-result snippet only); Wikipedia says the origin is unknown and the present form is mostly 19th-century. Start time: 9.52 pm (Wikipedia) versus 9.53 pm in some guides; ends at 10 pm. The wording follows the monarch: 'Queen Elizabeth's keys' before 2022. A bomb wounded part of the escort on 16 April 1941 but the ceremony was completed.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ceremony_of_the_Keys_(London) (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_tower_jewels" title="The Tower of London" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Which adventurer tried to steal the Crown Jewels in the 1670s?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Captain Kidd</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Guy Fawkes</Distractor>
      <Key>Thomas Blood (about 1671)</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Crown Jewels are kept in the Jewel House at the Tower. After the monarchy was abolished in 1649 most of the medieval regalia were sold or melted; most of the current collection dates from about 1660. St Edward's Crown was made in 1660-61. Thomas Blood stole a crown, a sceptre and an orb about 1671 and was caught. The collection holds around 23,578 gemstones including Cullinan I (530 carats) in the Sovereign's Sceptre and the Koh-i-Noor (105 carats).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Wikipedia dates the theft to two years after the jewels went on display in 1669, so about 1671. Blood attacked the 77-year-old deputy keeper. The Imperial State Crown (made 1937) holds Cullinan II (317 carats) and the Black Prince's Ruby, which is a spinel.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crown_Jewels_of_the_United_Kingdom (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

