<CapaProblem id="uk_speakers_origins" title="Hyde Park: Speakers' Corner" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What gallows once stood near Speakers' Corner?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Newgate</Distractor>
      <Key>Tyburn</Key>
      <Distractor>Smithfield</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The site is near the former Tyburn gallows, where condemned prisoners could make a last speech. The Reform League demonstration of 1866, in which crowds tore up railings, led to three days of rioting; in 1867 a crowd of 150,000 defied another ban and Home Secretary Spencer Walpole resigned the next day. Suffragettes held meetings there in 1906-14, including 250,000 on 21 June 1908.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Wikipedia adds 1855 Sunday Trading Bill riots (Marx: 'the beginning of the English revolution'); it describes 1866-67 as demonstrations.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.royalparks.org.uk/visit/parks/hyde-park/speakers-corner (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_speakers_rules" title="Hyde Park: Speakers' Corner" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What rule applies to speakers at Speakers' Corner?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>Any subject, provided the police consider it lawful</Key>
      <Distractor>Speakers must register a week in advance</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Only political subjects</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Anyone may turn up unannounced to speak on any subject as long as the police consider the speech lawful. Famous speakers include Karl Marx, Vladimir Lenin, George Orwell and Marcus Garvey, and Sunday mornings are busiest. Of about one hundred speaking places in London between 1855 and 1939, it is the last to survive.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.royalparks.org.uk/visit/parks/hyde-park/speakers-corner (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

