<CapaProblem id="uk_pp_overnight" title="Kensington Gardens: Peter Pan statue" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>How did Barrie arrange for the statue to appear in 1912?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>It was installed secretly overnight, appearing on May Day morning (1 May 1912)</Key>
      <Distractor>It was delivered by barge in daylight on 1 May</Distractor>
      <Distractor>It was unveiled at a public ceremony by the Lord Mayor</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The statue was erected overnight on 30 April 1912, without fanfare and without permission, so that children would find it on May Day morning and believe the fairies had put it there. The Times of 1 May announced 'a May-day gift by Mr J.M. Barrie, a figure of Peter Pan blowing his pipe on the stump of a tree'.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>CONFLICT on date: Wikipedia says 30 April 1912; LASSCO says installed on May Day itself; Historic England says overnight on the eve of May Day. Safest: erected overnight, discovered on 1 May 1912. Critics accused Barrie of advertising his play in a public park.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Peter_Pan_statue (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pp_listing_copies" title="Kensington Gardens: Peter Pan statue" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
