    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The current Black Rod is Lieutenant General Ed Davis, introduced to the House of Lords on 9 July 2025. His predecessor was Sarah Clarke, the first Lady Usher of the Black Rod (2018 to 2025).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Lords Hansard 9 July 2025: the King appointed Lt Gen Ed Davis Gentleman Usher of the Black Rod in succession to Sarah Clarke, who served over seven years. Maiden speeches up to 27 October 2026 still thank Black Rod and "his" team. No parliament.uk page naming the holder was found, and the hansard.parliament.uk page itself returned 403. "First Royal Marine" in the earlier draft was not confirmed and is dropped. Office holders change; recheck before publication.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://hansard-api.parliament.uk/search/contributions/Spoken.json?queryParameters.searchTerm=%22Black%20Rod%22%20Davis&amp;queryParameters.house=Lords (confidence: medium)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_blackrod_role" title="Black Rod" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Black Rod is the equivalent in the House of Lords of which Commons officer?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Speaker</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Lord Chancellor</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Clerk of the House</Distractor>
      <Key>The Serjeant at Arms</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Black Rod is also Serjeant-at-Arms of the House of Lords and Secretary to the Lord Great Chamberlain, and controls access to and keeps order in the Lords chamber and its precincts.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>parliament.uk says three offices are rolled into one in Black Rod; Black Rod's deputy is the Yeoman Usher.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/evolutionofparliament/parliamentwork/offices-and-ceremonies/overview/black-rod/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_blackrod_staff" title="Black Rod" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What is Black Rod's staff of office made of?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>Ebony (topped with a gold lion)</Key>
      <Distractor>Oak</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Silver gilt throughout</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Yew</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Black Rod's staff of office is an ebony rod, three and a half feet long, decorated with a gold lion; the present rod dates from 1883.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The rod also carries the Garter motto "Honi soit qui mal y pense" and a gold orb.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/evolutionofparliament/parliamentwork/offices-and-ceremonies/overview/black-rod/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_serjeant_role" title="The Serjeant at Arms and the Mace" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What is the role of the Serjeant at Arms in the House of Commons?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Writing the King's Speech</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Chairing debates</Distractor>
      <Key>Keeping order in the chamber and precincts; carrying the Mace before the Speaker</Key>
      <Distractor>Counting votes in divisions</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Serjeant at Arms of the House of Commons is responsible for order in the chamber, galleries and committees, and carries the Mace. The office dates from 1415; the monarch appointed the Serjeant until 1962, when the Commons' right to choose was restored.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The earlier draft said the Serjeant "is appointed by the Crown"; parliament.uk Living Heritage (https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/evolutionofparliament/parliamentwork/offices-and-ceremonies/overview/serjeant-at-arms/) says Crown appointment ran from Henry VIII to 1962. The Serjeant is the only person in the Commons allowed to carry a sword (glossary).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/mps-and-lords/principal/serjeant/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_serjeant_carries_mace" title="The Serjeant at Arms and the Mace" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Who carries the Mace into the Commons chamber at the start of each sitting?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Clerk of the House</Distractor>
      <Key>The Serjeant at Arms</Key>
      <Distractor>Black Rod</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Lord Chancellor</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Serjeant at Arms carries the Mace at the head of the Speaker's procession into the chamber before each sitting.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The Serjeant also carries the Mace into the House of Lords at the State Opening (parliament.uk Serjeant at Arms page).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/mace/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_serjeant_holder" title="The Serjeant at Arms and the Mace" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Who is the Serjeant at Arms of the House of Commons?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>Ugbana Oyet</Key>
      <Distractor>Sarah Clarke</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Lieutenant General Ed Davis</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Sir Lindsay Hoyle</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Ugbana Oyet is the Serjeant at Arms of the House of Commons, appointed in October 2019.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Confirmed on parliament.uk on 2026-10-30. Office holders change; recheck before publication.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/mps-and-lords/principal/serjeant/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_mace_what" title="The Serjeant at Arms and the Mace" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What is the Mace?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>A ceremonial silver-gilt staff symbolising royal authority, without which the House cannot meet</Key>
      <Distractor>The Speaker's gavel</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The key to the Commons door</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Sovereign's sceptre</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Mace is the symbol of royal authority in Parliament. The House of Commons mace is a silver-gilt ornamental club about five feet long, dating from the reign of Charles II.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/mace/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_mace_rests" title="The Serjeant at Arms and the Mace" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Where does the Mace rest during a sitting of the Commons?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>In the Speaker's hand</Distractor>
      <Key>On the Table of the House</Key>
      <Distractor>Across the Bar</Distractor>
      <Distractor>On the Woolsack</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>During a sitting the Mace rests on the Table of the House, in front of the Speaker. When the House is in committee it rests on two brackets underneath the Table.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/mace/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_mace_constituted" title="The Serjeant at Arms and the Mace" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What must be on the Table for the House of Commons to be properly constituted?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>The Mace</Key>
      <Distractor>The Woolsack</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Speaker's wig</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Royal Standard</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Without the Mace neither House of Parliament can meet or pass laws; its presence on the Table is the sign that the Commons is properly constituted.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The idea that the House is not properly constituted without the mace took hold in the 17th century (parliament.uk Living Heritage, Serjeant at Arms).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/mace/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_mace_lords" title="The Serjeant at Arms and the Mace" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Does the House of Lords have a Mace?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Yes, it lies on the Table</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Yes, it is kept in the Robing Room</Distractor>
      <Key>Yes; one of its two maces is placed on the Woolsack when the Lords sit</Key>
      <Distractor>No, only the Commons has one</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The House of Lords has two maces of its own, from the reigns of Charles II and William III. One accompanies the Lord Speaker into the chamber and is placed on the Woolsack whenever the House meets.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The earlier draft said Black Rod carries it; parliament.uk says the Deputy Serjeant-at-Arms or Principal Doorkeeper bears the Mace in the Lord Speaker's procession, and Black Rod joins at the end. The mace is absent from the Lords at the State Opening, when the Monarch is present.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/mace/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_woolsack_what" title="The Woolsack" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What is the Woolsack?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The throne of the monarch</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Mace stand</Distractor>
      <Key>The seat of the presiding officer of the House of Lords, a large cushion stuffed with wool</Key>
      <Distractor>The Speaker's chair in the Commons</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Woolsack is the seat of the Lord Speaker in the House of Lords: a large square cushion of wool covered in red cloth.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The earlier draft's "without back or arms" was not on any page read and is dropped.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/woolsack/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_woolsack_holder" title="The Woolsack" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Who sits on the Woolsack today?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Speaker of the Commons</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Prime Minister</Distractor>
      <Key>The Lord Speaker (currently Lord Forsyth of Drumlean)</Key>
      <Distractor>The Lord Chancellor</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Lord Speaker presides from the Woolsack; the office was created by the Constitutional Reform Act 2005 and first filled in July 2006, before which the Lord Chancellor presided. The current Lord Speaker is Lord Forsyth of Drumlean, elected in 2026, the fifth holder.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The earlier draft named Lord McFall of Alcluith, Lord Speaker 2021 to early 2026. Lords Hansard of 12 January 2026 records Forsyth's election, taking up the post the next month. Deputy Speakers sit on the Woolsack in his absence. Office holders change; recheck before publication.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/mps-and-lords/principal/lord-speaker/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_woolsack_wool" title="The Woolsack" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What is the Woolsack stuffed with?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Horsehair only</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Feathers</Distractor>
      <Key>Wool from the UK and Commonwealth countries</Key>
      <Distractor>Straw</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Woolsack's stuffing had changed over the years to hair; in 1938 it was re-stuffed with wool from England, Wales, Scotland, Northern Ireland and countries of the Commonwealth.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The wool was given by the International Wool Secretariat. The earlier draft's "15 Commonwealth countries" is not on parliament.uk and is dropped. Two further woolsacks seat the judges at the State Opening.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/living-heritage/building/palace/architecture/palace-s-interiors/lords-chamber/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_woolsack_why" title="The Woolsack" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Why is the Woolsack made of wool?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Because a Speaker was once a wool merchant</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Because wool is soft for long debates</Distractor>
      <Key>Wool was the basis of England's medieval wealth; the tradition dates from Edward III</Key>
      <Distractor>Because sheep were once kept in the chamber</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Woolsack dates from the reign of Edward III in the 14th century, when the wool trade was one of the most important parts of England's economy; a seat stuffed with wool symbolised the country's wealth.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The earlier draft's detail that Edward III had his Lord Chancellor sit on a wool bale in council is from Wikipedia and is not on parliament.uk.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/site-information/glossary/woolsack/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_components" title="The three components of Parliament" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What are the three components of the UK Parliament?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Crown, the Privy Council and the Commons</Distractor>
      <Key>The Sovereign (the Crown), the House of Lords, the House of Commons</Key>
      <Distractor>The Lords, the Commons and the courts</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Prime Minister, the Cabinet and the Speaker</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The UK Parliament has three components: the Sovereign, the House of Lords and the House of Commons.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>parliament.uk calls the State Opening the only regular occasion when the three constituent parts meet. Already in facts.yaml as parl-components; dedupe on merge.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/occasions/stateopening/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_bill_stages" title="How a bill becomes an Act" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What are the stages of a bill in the first House?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>First reading, second reading, committee stage, report stage, third reading</Key>
      <Distractor>Consultation, debate, veto</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Draft, vote, assent</Distractor>
      <Distractor>First reading, committee, Royal Assent, report</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>A bill goes through first reading, second reading, committee stage, report stage, third reading, then the other House, and finally Royal Assent.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>First reading is a formality without debate; amendments cannot be made at third reading in the Commons. Read on parliament.uk's first, committee and third reading pages.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/laws/passage-bill/commons/coms-commons-comittee-stage/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_assent" title="How a bill becomes an Act" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What turns a bill into an Act of Parliament?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>A vote of the Privy Council</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The third reading</Distractor>
      <Key>Royal Assent</Key>
      <Distractor>Signature by the Prime Minister</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>A bill becomes an Act of Parliament when it receives Royal Assent from the Sovereign after both Houses agree it.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The parliament.uk glossary says assent was last refused in 1708 (under Queen Anne) and is now a formality.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/laws/passage-bill/commons/coms-royal-assent/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_bill_other_house" title="How a bill becomes an Act" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What happens to a bill after its third reading in the Commons?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>It goes to the House of Lords, which goes through the same stages</Key>
      <Distractor>It goes to a referendum</Distractor>
      <Distractor>It is sent to the Cabinet</Distractor>
      <Distractor>It goes straight to the King</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>After its third reading a bill passes to the other House, which repeats the stages; both Houses must agree the same text before Royal Assent.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/laws/passage-bill/ says a bill must be approved in the same form by both Houses.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/laws/passage-bill/commons/coms-commons-third-reading/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_petitions" title="Petitions" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Who can formally present a paper petition to the House of Commons?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>A Member of Parliament</Key>
      <Distractor>Black Rod</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Speaker alone</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Any citizen who walks into the chamber</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>A paper petition to the House of Commons must be presented by a Member of Parliament, who may do so in the chamber or by placing it in the petitions bag behind the Speaker's chair; e-petitions are separate.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>MPs are not obliged to present a petition. Petitions to the House of Lords are presented by members of the Lords. E-petitions get a Government response at 10,000 signatures and may be debated at 100,000 (https://petition.parliament.uk/help).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/get-involved/sign-a-petition/paper-petitions/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_prorogation" title="Prorogation" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What happens to bills that have not received Royal Assent when Parliament is prorogued?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>They automatically become Acts</Distractor>
      <Distractor>They go to the Supreme Court</Distractor>
      <Key>They fall (are lost) unless a carry-over motion lets them continue into the next session</Key>
      <Distractor>They continue unchanged</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Prorogation ends a parliamentary session. The King prorogues Parliament on the advice of the Privy Council, and bills not yet given Royal Assent fall unless carried over.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The first bill carried over was the Financial Services and Markets Bill in 1998-99. Carry-over lasts 12 months from first reading and cannot be done twice [VERIFY, not on the pages read].</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/occasions/prorogation/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_dev_scotland" title="Devolution" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>When did the Scottish Parliament first meet?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Key>1999</Key>
      <Distractor>1998</Distractor>
      <Distractor>2007</Distractor>
      <Distractor>1707</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Scotland Act 1998 created the Scottish Parliament, after a 1997 referendum; it first met on 12 May 1999.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The Scottish Parliament took up its devolved powers on 1 July 1999 (https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/role/relations-with-other-institutions/devolved/).</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.scot/about/history-of-the-scottish-parliament (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_dev_wales" title="Devolution" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>What was the National Assembly for Wales renamed in 2020?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Welsh Executive</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Tŷ Cymru</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Cynulliad Cymru</Distractor>
      <Key>Senedd Cymru (the Welsh Parliament)</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The National Assembly for Wales was created by the Government of Wales Act 1998 (first met 1999). It was renamed Senedd Cymru (the Welsh Parliament) on 6 May 2020.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>The exact date 6 May 2020 is from https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/role/relations-with-other-institutions/devolved/; the Senedd page gives the year and the 1997 referendum.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://senedd.wales/how-we-work/history-of-devolution/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_dev_ni" title="Devolution" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Which 1998 agreement led to the Northern Ireland Assembly?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>The Downing Street Declaration</Distractor>
      <Key>The Belfast Agreement (Good Friday Agreement)</Key>
      <Distractor>The Anglo-Irish Treaty</Distractor>
      <Distractor>The Act of Union</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>The Northern Ireland Assembly was set up after the Belfast Agreement, reached on Good Friday, 10 April 1998.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>Direct Rule ended on 2 December 1999. The earlier Home Rule parliament of 1921 to 1972 is from Wikipedia [VERIFY].</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://education.niassembly.gov.uk/years-11-12/evolution-devolution (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_dev_reserved" title="Devolution" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Name a reserved matter that the UK Parliament, not the Scottish Parliament, controls.</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Health</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Local government</Distractor>
      <Key>Defence (or foreign policy)</Key>
      <Distractor>Education</Distractor>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>Reserved matters, including defence and foreign policy, stay with the UK Parliament; devolved matters such as education and health are decided by the devolved bodies. Devolution is not federalism: the UK Parliament keeps authority over the devolved institutions and can still legislate on devolved matters, though it generally does not.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/role/relations-with-other-institutions/devolved/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

<CapaProblem id="uk_pol_dev_england" title="Devolution" dontKnow="true">
  <KeyGrader>
    <Markdown>Which of the four countries has no devolved parliament or assembly of its own?</Markdown>
    <ChoiceInput>
      <Distractor>Northern Ireland</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Wales</Distractor>
      <Distractor>Scotland</Distractor>
      <Key>England</Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Explanation>
      <Markdown>England has no devolved parliament; English matters are legislated for by the UK Parliament.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>parliament.uk lists devolved bodies for Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland only.</Markdown>
      <Markdown>*Source: https://www.parliament.uk/about/how/role/relations-with-other-institutions/devolved/ (confidence: high)*</Markdown>
    </Explanation>
  </KeyGrader>
</CapaProblem>

